{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793651765,"nonce":"LengWvsOzrT6pDrwdHzYMuwCzuM=","title":"Preserve the stack across Win64 dynamic callbacks","message":"RayDE distribution run 41417311722 at 1867e7dda0b24acf82753bdd6ab4a815ff09ae32 builds every native target; Windows fails --check-install with 0xC0000005 and no output. Every bundled DLL loader probe passes. The saved-archive diagnostic run 41520026431 confirms libhl!ash_static_call+0x26c, with Child-SP 0x00000000000000d3 and the debugger warning that frames are outside the stack bounds.\n\nThe Windows bridge in std/src/fun.rs saves rsp into r11, aligns and allocates shadow space, calls r10, then restores rsp from r11. r11 is volatile on Win64, so the callee may overwrite the saved stack pointer. The inline comment incorrectly says volatility makes it suitable to preserve the pointer across a call. The sequence is present both in RayDE's pinned Ash 40894c76149c286e3d1430f7c436f63a769cd0e9 and current Ash main 001b3da9.\n\nMove the Win64 call into a bridge with a real fixed stack frame, correct Windows unwind information and preserved return registers. Keep the outgoing shadow space and any stack arguments separate from saved state. Add a regression where the callee explicitly clobbers r11, mixed float/integer arguments and spilled arguments; run static and shared AOT startup on Windows. Do not simply save rsp in another volatile register. Review exception/unwind handling at the same time (the SysV fix 50a7904d covers a different cfg arm).\n\nEvidence: https://github.com/rayzor-blade/RayDE/actions/runs/41520026431 . The windows-startup-crash artifact holds debugger.txt and events.txt; it contains no memory dump. Parent RayDE distribution tracker: 819fd7e. Related portable-target adoption: 4a4217e.","files":null}]}